review: W-026 (parallel workstreams: per-opus branching) — round 2
sella: qa-lead
verdict: FAIL

Round 2 exists because round 1 ran on a censor below the tier D-014 requires
for a review gate. Every round-1 claim was re-tested from the code, not taken
on trust. Nothing in W-026 changed between rounds (HEAD 1b975c8, clean tree).

Base state verified at HEAD 1b975c8:
- npm test exit 0 (full suite).
- npm run -s typecheck exit 0.
- npm run -s lint exit 0, 7 warnings; two of them are W-026's own
  (branch.test.ts unused `mkdirSync`, dead `repo`). Certificates honest.
- node --import tsx packages/cli/src/branch.test.ts . -> 10/10 PASS.

Round 1 findings, re-tested
---------------------------

1. CONFIRMED (blocking). Behaviour 4 ("mergeOpusBranch refuses when opus
   state is not `done`") is absent. branch.ts reads no opus file at all;
   runMerge only existsSync's studio/opera/<id>.md and never looks at
   `state:`. branch.test.ts's "behaviour 4" block asserts "refuses missing
   branch" — a requirement nobody wrote — so the claimed 7-behaviour suite
   is 6 behaviours plus a substitute. `bisellium merge` will merge an opus
   in `building` today.
   Note for the fix: the guard needs no new machinery. `splitFront` in
   packages/commands/src/frontmatter.ts already parses opus front matter,
   and check.ts:468 (state.done.probationes) already blocks `state: done`
   with an unpassed gate — so a `state === "done"` test subsumes the
   "after gates pass" half of the interface line for free.

2. PARTLY OVERTURNED. Round 1 treated "merge after gates pass, push, delete
   branch" as one half-built interface. Split it:
   - gates: not an independent finding. `done` already implies gates passed
     (check.ts:468). It collapses into finding 1.
   - push: real gap against the Interfaces line (no `git push` in
     branch.ts; the only `push` is Array.push at branch.ts:77) but I do not
     hold it blocking for this opus. No behaviour in the brief lists it, the
     acceptance criterion does not need it, and this repo has a live remote
     (origin git@github.com:edckt/bisellium.git) — a merge helper that
     silently pushes to it is an architect's decision, not something a
     builder should infer from one clause. Downgraded to ADVISORY: the brief
     overstates its own testable behaviours; either drop `push` from the
     Interfaces line or add it as behaviour 8 with a test.

3. CONFIRMED (blocking). `bisellium run --opus <id>` (behaviour 7) has no
   test anywhere. Grepped every *.ts under packages/, apps/, adapters/ for
   `--opus`: the only test hits are writes.test.ts exercising handoff/emit,
   an unrelated flag. The 10 runCommand cases in packages/shim/test/
   shim.test.ts all pass --no-worktree, so neither run.ts:147 (the
   --opus/--base mutual-exclusion guard) nor run.ts:148 (`base =
   opus/${opus}`) is executed by any test — the worktree/base path is never
   entered with a base at all.

4. CONFIRMED (blocking), reproduced independently. Copied branch.ts and
   branch.test.ts to a scratch dir, inverted `if (canMerge.status !== 0)` to
   `=== 0` (branch.ts:49), ran the suite: 10/10 PASS, exit 0. The only
   observable difference is the detail string printed by behaviour 5, which
   flips from "branch opus/W-031 conflicts with master" to "branch
   opus/W-031 has diverged from master — rebase first". Both arms return
   `{ ok: false }` and the test asserts only `result.ok === false`, so the
   `git merge --no-commit --no-ff` conflict probe is dead code from the
   suite's point of view. Round 1's account is exactly right.

New in round 2
--------------

5. NEW, BLOCKING. mergeOpusBranch merges into whatever HEAD points at — it
   never names master. The string "master" does not occur anywhere in
   branch.ts; merge-base is taken against HEAD (branch.ts:44-46) and the
   merge is `git merge --ff-only <branch>` into the current checkout, while
   runMerge prints "merged opus/<id> into master" unconditionally
   (branch.ts:114).
   Probe (temp repo, opus commit on opus/W-A, then `git checkout -b
   sidebranch` before merging):
     result: {"ok":true}
     master log:      0f940ec init            <- untouched
     sidebranch log:  727685f opus work | 0f940ec init
   A merge run from the wrong checkout silently lands on the wrong branch
   and reports success against master. For an opus whose whole purpose is
   keeping concurrent workstreams off each other, this is the central risk,
   and no test pins the target branch.

6. NEW, BLOCKING. Running `merge` from the opus branch itself is a silent
   no-op reported as success — and that is the likely operator path, since
   builders work in a worktree checked out on opus/<id>. HEAD == merge-base,
   so `git merge --ff-only opus/<id>` returns "Already up to date" (status
   0), then `git branch -d` fails (cannot delete the current branch) and its
   return value is discarded (branch.ts:60).
   Probe (opus commit made on opus/W-G2, merge invoked without leaving it):
     result: {"ok":true}   branches after: master,opus/W-G2
     master log: still at init
   ok:true, "merged opus/W-G2 into master" printed, master not advanced,
   branch not deleted. Behaviour 6's test only ever runs from master, so it
   cannot see this.

7. NEW. No clean-tree guard, and uncommitted work is misdiagnosed as a
   conflict. The divergence path runs `git merge --no-commit --no-ff` and
   then `git merge --abort` in the operator's live working tree — it mutates
   the tree to answer a question that `git merge-tree` answers without
   touching it.
   Probe (master moved, opus branch edits shared.ts, no actual conflict
   between the two branches, one uncommitted local edit to shared.ts):
     result: {"ok":false,"error":"branch opus/W-B2 conflicts with master"}
   There is no conflict; git refused to start the merge because of the
   local change, and the error text blames the branch. The uncommitted work
   survived in this probe, but a probe that aborts a merge in a dirty tree
   is one step from losing it, and nothing in the suite covers a dirty tree.

8. NEW. The acceptance criterion has no test and is not met as shipped.
   "Two opera can be worked concurrently in separate sessions, each on its
   own branch, and merged independently when done" — no test merges two
   opus branches, and under the ff-only model the second one cannot merge
   after the first lands: master has moved, so mergeOpusBranch returns
   "has diverged from master — rebase first" even with zero conflicts.
   W-026 ships nothing to perform that rebase. Either the acceptance line or
   the fast-forward-only model needs the architect; it is not a builder's
   call.

9. ADVISORY. branch.test.ts:11 `const repo = resolve(process.argv[2] ?? ".")`
   is never read, and `cpSync`/`mkdirSync` are imported unused. The suite
   advertises a repo argument it ignores; every case builds its own temp
   repo. Cosmetic, but it is why the file reads as testing the real repo
   when it does not.

10. ADVISORY. createOpusBranch forks from HEAD (Interfaces line) while the
    brief's Intent says branches are "forked from master". Same root as
    findings 5 and 6: no code path in branch.ts ever names master. Test 1
    only runs from master, so the divergence is invisible to the suite.

11. Bookkeeping, outside W-026's code. studio/opera/W-026.md records the
    round-1 review as `sella: eng-lead`, while ci/W-026-review-1.log's own
    header says `sella: qa-lead`. One of the two is wrong; for the clerk.

Verdict
-------

FAIL. Back to building. Blocking: 1 (done-state guard, untested and
unimplemented), 3 (run --opus has no test), 4 (conflict probe is dead code —
assert the error text, not just .ok), 5 (merge targets HEAD, not master),
6 (merge from the opus branch is a false success). Advisory: 2-push, 7, 9,
10. Finding 8 is a question for the architect before the next build round,
not a defect to hand a builder.

Commands run for this log:
  git log --oneline -1; git status --porcelain
  node --import tsx packages/cli/src/branch.test.ts .
  npm test; npm run -s typecheck; npm run -s lint
  sed-mutated copy of branch.ts+branch.test.ts in a scratch dir, suite re-run
  three temp-repo probes driving createOpusBranch/mergeOpusBranch directly
  (wrong-checkout merge, merge from the opus branch, dirty tree + divergence)
